

By default, Transformers.js uses [hosted pretrained models](https://huggingface.co/models?library=transformers.js) and [precompiled WASM binaries](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@huggingface/transformers@4.0.0-next.1/dist/), which should work out-of-the-box. You can customize this as follows:

### Settings

```javascript
import { env } from '@huggingface/transformers';

// Specify a custom location for models (defaults to '/models/').
env.localModelPath = '/path/to/models/';

// Disable the loading of remote models from the Hugging Face Hub:
env.allowRemoteModels = false;

// Set location of .wasm files. Defaults to use a CDN.
env.backends.onnx.wasm.wasmPaths = '/path/to/files/';
```

For a full list of available settings, check out the [API Reference](./api/env).

### Grammar-constrained generation (`generate({ grammar: "..." })`)

You can constrain decoding by passing a grammar string to `generate`:

```javascript
const outputs = await model.generate({
  ...inputs,
  max_new_tokens: 80,
  grammar: `
root ::= object
object ::= "{" ws "\\\"name\\\"" ws ":" ws string ws "," ws "\\\"age\\\"" ws ":" ws number ws "}"
string ::= "\\\"" [a-zA-Z ]+ "\\\""
number ::= [0-9]+
ws ::= [ \t\n\r]*
`,
});
```

This is useful for enforcing structured outputs (for example JSON or a small command language).

#### Supported grammar features (GBNF subset)

Transformers.js supports a practical GBNF subset for constrained decoding:

- Rule definitions: `rule ::= ...`
- Alternation: `a | b`
- Grouping: `( ... )`
- Repetition operators: `*`, `+`, `?`
- Character classes / ranges: `[a-z]`, `[0-9]`
- String literals: `"open"`, `"{"`, etc.
- Optional whitespace helper rules (for example `ws ::= [ \t\n\r]*`)

#### Token terminals and caveats

In addition to text terminals, token-level terminals are available:

- `<[1234]>`: match an explicit token id.
- `<token>`: match a named token (if available in the tokenizer vocabulary).
- Negation (`!`) can be used with terminals/classes to ban a match.

Caveats:

- Tokenization is model-specific, so token terminal behavior can vary across models.
- Prefer text-level rules unless you need token-exact control.
- Escaping can be subtle (for example `"\\\""` for a literal quote in JavaScript strings).

#### Failure behavior when the grammar is impossible

If no valid next token satisfies both the model distribution and grammar, generation fails fast and the `generate(...)` call rejects with an error.
In production, catch this error and retry with a relaxed grammar, larger `max_new_tokens`, or a fallback unconstrained decode path.

#### Performance implications

Grammar constraints add per-token filtering work during decoding. In practice:

- Throughput is lower than unconstrained generation.
- Overhead increases with grammar complexity (deep nesting, many alternatives, heavy token-terminal usage).
- For best performance, keep grammars small, deterministic, and avoid unnecessary ambiguity.

Example of a tiny command grammar:

```bnf
root ::= command
command ::= "open" ws target | "delete" ws target | "list"
target ::= "file" | "folder"
ws ::= " "
```

### Convert your models to ONNX

We recommend using [Optimum](https://github.com/huggingface/optimum-onnx) to convert your PyTorch models to ONNX in a single command. For the full list of supported architectures, check out the [Optimum documentation](https://huggingface.co/docs/optimum-onnx/onnx/overview).
